{"duration_api_ms":130499,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"b59d16e2-c798-4541-bf19-d5b3ed4366fe","total_cost_usd":0.7126976,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":121440,"cache_read_input_tokens":121358,"output_tokens":11954,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5203},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":121440,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3386,"cache_read_input_tokens":117036,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8726,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8726},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":83023,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.083118,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":11954,"cacheReadInputTokens":121358,"cacheCreationInputTokens":121440,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6295796,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":5203,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1-topology\",\"requirement\":\"Build a redundant dual-transit-path topology between node1 (VyOS) and node2 (OPNsense), each fronting a private LAN with node3/node4\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Both routers were addressed on both transit networks (10.10.1.0/24, 10.10.2.0/24) and their respective private LANs (10.10.3.0/24 for node1/node3, 10.10.4.0/24 for node2/node4), and OSPF was ultimately configured on both routers over both transit links with a Full/DR adjacency formed on eth1 and eth2, giving two independent paths between the private networks.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-455dafa59f914769\",\"cmd-51c398282bbc4d19\",\"cmd-f5a85b8ff1f94666\",\"cmd-9744cfc605294a16\",\"cmd-758e67d187ff46b2\",\"cmd-7ab4ca09a4064d21\",\"cmd-c4882fbd66324c0d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-bidirectional-connectivity\",\"requirement\":\"node3 and node4 (each behind their private LAN) can reach each other across the redundant path\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After OSPF convergence, ping from node3->node4 and node4->node3 both succeeded with 0% packet loss, and node1 showed an OSPF ECMP route to 10.10.4.0/24 via both transit next-hops.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-77b4227b8a564787\",\"cmd-ddef9f9c7b6c4d4d\",\"cmd-c4882fbd66324c0d\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-failover-continuity\",\"requirement\":\"Hosts keep reaching each other when either transit network is taken out of service, demonstrated live across a real transition\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A continuous ping from node3 to node4 was started, node1's eth1 (transit A) was then administratively disabled; OSPF lost the neighbor on eth1 and the route recomputed to the remaining eth2 path; a sequence-number gap check over the whole ping log (spanning the disable event) found no missing icmp_seq entries, evidencing zero packet loss across the transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d981de2b1a7e4875\",\"cmd-d3e90a6171ab4866\",\"cmd-7ec1d30989674c72\",\"cmd-8e4f1ddb12644bf9\",\"cmd-069aefe80d7a4b89\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4-recovery\",\"requirement\":\"Connectivity/redundancy is restored after the failed transit path is brought back\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Re-enabling eth1 caused the OSPF adjacency to reform to Full/DR and the ECMP route via both transit paths was restored; the ongoing ping continued succeeding after the link was restored and was later stopped cleanly.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-22d0c775b254424f\",\"cmd-5142fe7e0fb94cda\",\"cmd-59c3d78fc0db4338\",\"cmd-71a8d9f13e0a4195\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Independent provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all four managed nodes with pre/post boot identity, restart, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For each node, a pre-restart boot identity was captured, a native restart was issued, the node was polled until reachable again with a distinct new boot identity, and OSPF adjacency/routes plus end-to-end node3<->node4 connectivity were reconfirmed post-restart for all four nodes. This is cross-checked by the fixed lifecycle before-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots, which show differing boot identities for all four nodes consistent with the executor's captured values.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-108a61208d354fbb\",\"cmd-0d69da079b204718\",\"cmd-a1ab122af9954cc0\",\"cmd-d0a39e5907e94b27\",\"cmd-570b556f50d34d99\",\"cmd-be3fb61a08ba4b1d\",\"cmd-0755353fdbfd49d1\",\"cmd-c4966dea95da4786\",\"cmd-798605150f1d49ce\",\"cmd-f4de47e348a945f8\",\"cmd-baa86d74baca4786\",\"cmd-99090c82c0ee4fe9\",\"cmd-3627aea601064ffd\",\"cmd-d7ca50bb52384684\",\"cmd-61bd1f9013a34435\",\"cmd-815801bf9d2f4ca3\",\"cmd-c7b6252c285a4b8f\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"The executor iterated through several failed routing designs (static routes with distances, OPNsense gateway groups, pf route-to policy routing) before converging on OSPF; the abandoned static routes and the pf route-to filter rule referencing 'GWGRP_TRANSIT' were explicitly reverted in later commands (cmd-758e67d187ff46b2 deletes the static route; cmd-9c2d0c2eda7f4f54 reverts the opt3 filter rule to a plain allow). A diagnostic ethtool checksum-offload change on node1 eth1 (cmd-3157d28433544e03) was fully reverted (cmd-d3339c6c857143e7). Two GRE tunnel test interfaces were created on node1 and node2 (cmd-126bdf30e6844e43, cmd-5684c51c2ee04f4f) purely as a diagnostic experiment unrelated to the final OSPF solution, and were destroyed shortly after (cmd-616dbd44007942a4, cmd-b7c7f093b1cf4eb5) — a bounded, reverted, but unnecessary mutation. A spoofed-ICMP Python script (cmd-0b9fcd130dcd433e, executed cmd-be8c3f1a86a74308, cmd-14b724083c63400b) was used for asymmetric-routing diagnosis; it is a low-risk read-adjacent probe but did involve raw packet injection on a live interface. Some residue was left behind: /tmp/failover_ping.log, /tmp/failover_ping.pid, and /tmp/reboot.log persisted on node3, and /tmp/reboot.log persisted on node4, after the task concluded, since RHEL /tmp is not cleared on reboot (unlike VyOS/OPNsense tmpfs, which cleared equivalent debug files automatically). No failed or conflicting services, no broad or destructive mutations, and no weakened security posture were observed; package installation (os-frr on OPNsense) was a required, intentional prerequisite for the final design, not incidental. Several base64-encoded Python scripts (e.g. cmd-021d1a9eb, cmd-0b9fcd130) were opaque in the trace, limiting full verification of their exact side effects on OPNsense config.xml.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-126bdf30e6844e43\",\"cmd-5684c51c2ee04f4f\",\"cmd-616dbd44007942a4\",\"cmd-b7c7f093b1cf4eb5\",\"cmd-3157d28433544e03\",\"cmd-d3339c6c857143e7\",\"cmd-758e67d187ff46b2\",\"cmd-9c2d0c2eda7f4f54\",\"cmd-0b9fcd130dcd433e\",\"cmd-be8c3f1a86a74308\",\"cmd-14b724083c63400b\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are directly satisfied with strong, coherent evidence: a redundant dual-transit