2c285a4b8f"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"The executor iterated through several failed routing designs (static routes with distances, OPNsense gateway groups, pf route-to policy routing) before converging on OSPF; the abandoned static routes and the pf route-to filter rule referencing 'GWGRP_TRANSIT' were explicitly reverted in later commands (cmd-758e67d187ff46b2 deletes the static route; cmd-9c2d0c2eda7f4f54 reverts the opt3 filter rule to a plain allow). A diagnostic ethtool checksum-offload change on node1 eth1 (cmd-3157d28433544e03) was fully reverted (cmd-d3339c6c857143e7). Two GRE tunnel test interfaces were created on node1 and node2 (cmd-126bdf30e6844e43, cmd-5684c51c2ee04f4f) purely as a diagnostic experiment unrelated to the final OSPF solution, and were destroyed shortly after (cmd-616dbd44007942a4, cmd-b7c7f093b1cf4eb5) — a bounded, reverted, but unnecessary mutation. A spoofed-ICMP Python script (cmd-0b9fcd130dcd433e, executed cmd-be8c3f1a86a74308, cmd-14b724083c63400b) was used for asymmetric-routing diagnosis; it is a low-risk read-adjacent probe but did involve raw packet injection on a live interface. Some residue was left behind: /tmp/failover_ping.log, /tmp/failover_ping.pid, and /tmp/reboot.log persisted on node3, and /tmp/reboot.log persisted on node4, after the task concluded, since RHEL /tmp is not cleared on reboot (unlike VyOS/OPNsense tmpfs, which cleared equivalent debug files automatically). No failed or conflicting services, no broad or destructive mutations, and no weakened security posture were observed; package installation (os-frr on OPNsense) was a required, intentional prerequisite for the final design, not incidental. Several base64-encoded Python scripts (e.g. cmd-021d1a9eb, cmd-0b9fcd130) were opaque in the trace, limiting full verification of their exact side effects on OPNsense config.xml.","evidence_ids":["cmd-126bdf30e6844e43","cmd-5684c51c2ee04f4f","cmd-616dbd44007942a4","cmd-b7c7f093b1cf4eb5","cmd-3157d28433544e03","cmd-d3339c6c857143e7","cmd-758e67d187ff46b2","cmd-9c2d0c2eda7f4f54","cmd-0b9fcd130dcd433e","cmd-be8c3f1a86a74308","cmd-14b724083c63400b","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are directly satisfied with strong, coherent evidence: a redundant dual-transit topology was built and finally implemented via OSPF (after discarding a failed static-route/gateway-group/pf route-to design), bidirectional node3<->node4 connectivity was verified, a live failover across a real transit-network disruption showed zero packet loss (verified via sequence-gap analysis of the ping log), recovery after restoring the link was confirmed, and the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all four nodes with matching pre/post boot-identity changes cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. Hygiene is good but not perfect: several diagnostic experiments (GRE tunnels, an ethtool offload toggle, a spoofed-ICMP probe) were used during troubleshooting and mostly reverted, and small leftover log/pid files remain in /tmp on node3 and node4 (RHEL /tmp is not cleared on reboot, unlike the VyOS/OPNsense nodes).","limitations":["Several executor commands executed base64-encoded Python scripts against OPNsense's config.xml; their exact byte-level diffs were not directly visible in the trace, limiting full certainty about what intermediate state they introduced beyond what was later confirmed via config dumps.","Minor unreverted residue (/tmp/failover_ping.log, /tmp/failover_ping.pid, /tmp/reboot.log on node3; /tmp/reboot.log on node4) remains after the task, though it is low-risk and bounded.","The failover ping-log gap check (cmd-069aefe80d7a4b89) relies on the executor's own awk script output ('last seq: 111' with no GAP lines) rather than a fully rendered gapless sequence list, though this is a standard and verifiable method."]},"ttft_